{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## What this ticket is now (rewritten 2026-08-19 after a code-level review)\n\nThe original framing of this ticket was WRONG and has been replaced. It claimed ten\nmodel-emitted free-text `criteria` aliases were silently bypassing per-criterion routing, and\nproposed constraining the reviewer prompt/schema to the registry vocabulary. A read of the actual\ncode-review pipeline showed that the premise does not hold and that the proposed remedy would\nBREAK a working extensibility mechanism.\n\n### Why the original framing was wrong\n\n1. **`criteria` holds DIMENSION ids, and the vocabulary is open BY DESIGN.**\n   `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/code-review-base.md:33-34` instructs the base reviewer:\n   \"`criteria`: the code-review dimension id(s) the finding maps to (**e.g.** `correctness`,\n   `edge-cases`, `error-handling`, `maintainability`, `regression`)\". That is an open \"e.g.\" list,\n   not a closed enum. The pydantic contract agrees — `CodeFinding.criteria: list[str]`, described\n   as \"Code-review dimension/overlay id(s) the finding maps to\"\n   (`src/rebar/llm/code_review/contracts.py`).\n\n2. **Project-supplied criteria are a first-class feature, and the analysis missed them.**\n   `src/rebar/llm/code_review/registry.py` exposes a THREE-layer vocabulary:\n   `routing_index()` (packaged built-ins only), `effective_routing(repo_root)` (packaged MERGED\n   with the project's `.rebar/criteria_routing.json` `code_review` map), and\n   `effective_criteria(repo_root)` — \"the packaged built-ins union the project ids the overlay\n   activates (minus any disabled built-in)\". Any check of whether a label is known MUST use the\n   effective vocabulary, not the packaged index.\n\n3. **A fixed enum would break the system.** Constraining the schema to a static list would make\n   project-supplied criteria unexpressible and would also forbid the base reviewer's designed\n   open dimension vocabulary. This is explicitly NOT to be built.\n\n4. **The 0.95 default is intentional, not an accident.** `registry.py` documents\n   `DEFAULT_BLOCK_THRESHOLD = 0.95` as \"the kernel's default when a criterion has no routing\n   entry ... the high-threshold, mostly-advisory v1 stance\". Dimensions landing on it is the\n   designed posture.\n\n### Measured reality (code-v3 corpus, 1663 sidecars, 5669 label instances)\n\nOf the labels not present in the PACKAGED routing index, the overwhelming majority are correct:\n`maintainability` (869), `correctness` (808), `edge-cases` (553) are prompt-specified dimensions;\n`bugfix-size-attestation` (61) is a real criterion defined at\n`src/rebar/llm/code_review/bugfix_size_gate.py:45` (`CRITERION_ID`);\n`project.review-phase-boundaries` (10) is a real PROJECT criterion declared in this repo's\n`.rebar/criteria_routing.json` and activated for `code_review`. Only `sec` (8) and\n`documentation` (5) are genuine synonym sloppiness.\n\n## Scope — two items\n\n### Item 1 (APPROVED): normalize the two genuine synonyms\n`sec` and `documentation` are true synonyms of the packaged criteria `security` and `docs`.\nBecause they do not match a known criterion id they fall to the default posture instead of their\ncriterion's tuned threshold.\n\nNormalize them onto their canonical ids. The normalization MUST be a synonym MAPPING, not a\nwhitelist: a label that matches no known id must pass through UNCHANGED, so that open dimensions\nand project criteria are unaffected. Membership must be tested against the EFFECTIVE vocabulary\n(`effective_criteria(repo_root)` / `effective_routing(repo_root)`), never the packaged index.\n\n### Item 2 (POSTPONED — do not implement here)\nWhether `correctness` / `maintainability` / `edge-cases` should get their own routing entries so\nthey can be tuned per-dimension is a deliberate POSTURE change with real blast radius: those three\naccount for ~2230 findings currently sitting on the deliberately-high 0.95 default, so giving them\na normal threshold could materially increase what blocks a change. Operator decision 2026-08-19:\npostponed to a future session. Do NOT fold it into this ticket.\n\n### Item 3 (APPROVED): correct the calibration document — SCOPE EXPANDED\nAn independent validation pass (2026-08-19) confirmed all seven of this ticket's code claims AND\nfound Item 3 as originally written was UNDER-SCOPED. The mistake is not confined to one paragraph;\nit also reaches the document's load-bearing OUTPUT sections. All five sites must be corrected:\n\n**(A) lines 88 and 96-98 — `sec` is published as a deterministic/attestation gate. Sharpest error.**\nThe proposal table carries `| sec | 8 | DET/ATTEST | n/a | 0.00 | deterministic/attestation gate\n(pblk=1.0, validity~0); not LLM-tunable |`, and the adjudicated recommendation lists `sec` alongside\n`bugfix-size-attestation` and `secret-detection` as \"Not LLM-threshold-tunable\". `sec` is a\nmodel-emitted synonym of `security` — an ordinary LLM finding label. This DIRECTLY CONTRADICTS this\nticket's approved Item 1: the document currently stands as a published recommendation against the\nvery normalization we are about to do. Root cause is `calibrate_code_review_thresholds.py:224-226`,\nwhose DET branch fires on the purely statistical signature `p_block > 0 and validity < 0.05` and\nnever consults any routing index — so ANY label with blocked-but-unverified findings is misfiled.\n\n**(B) lines 57 and 88 — the `security` row is measurably understated.** `sec`'s 8 findings are split\nout of `security`'s 60, so that row's n, pblk (0.05 vs ~0.16 pooled), drop (0.267 vs ~0.235) and mval\nare computed on a ~12% truncated sample — and the doc then leans on exactly those numbers\n(\"Keep as-is: `security` @ 0.54 ... Borderline rev_rr (0.588)\"). Item 1's normalization invalidates\nthis row's inputs; it must be re-derived or carry an explicit caveat. `documentation`/`docs` is the\nsame defect at n=5/455 — numerically negligible, worth one sentence.\n\n**(C) line 78 — `project.review-phase-boundaries` is proposed at 0.95 \"LOW-DATA\"** when it ALREADY\nhas a tuned entry at `block_threshold: 0.9` in `.rebar/criteria_routing.json`. The script proposes a\nthreshold for a criterion whose threshold is already set, because it reads no routing at all.\n\n**(D) lines 135-136 — the label list is both mis-characterized AND under-inclusive.** It names ten\nlabels \"NOT in criteria_routing.json\" while omitting `maintainability`, `correctness`, `edge-cases`\nand `bugfix-size-attestation`, none of which is in the packaged index either (the packaged file has\nexactly 20 keys). Correct BOTH defects, not just the characterization.\n\n**(E) the generator, not only the prose.** `calibrate_code_review_thresholds.py` never reads\n`criteria_routing.json` at all, so any re-run reproduces (A) and (C) verbatim. A prose-only fix\nleaves the generator wrong. Record this even if the code change is deferred.\n\nFor every site: state that the comparison was made against the PACKAGED routing index rather than\nthe EFFECTIVE vocabulary; that dimension labels are open by design; that the 0.95 default is the\nintended v1 posture, not a bypass; and that constraining the prompt to a fixed vocabulary is\nREJECTED because it would break project criteria. Do not delete these sections — a corrected record\nis more useful than a removed one.\n\n**Bound the correction honestly.** The four approved blocking flips (`regression` 0.54,\n`api-compat` 0.51, `deletion-impact` 0.60, `error-handling` 0.50) and the block-impact table are\nNOT affected, and all four have already landed in the packaged routing index. Say so explicitly so\nthe next reader does not over-rotate and discard a calibration whose headline result still stands.\n\n### Item 4 (NEW — flagged by validation, needs an operator decision before Item 1 ships)\nNormalizing `documentation` -> `docs` is NOT purely hygienic. `docs` carries `nit_suppressed: true`\nin the packaged routing, and `workflow_ops.py:432,449` demotes an advisory finding to `dropped` when\nits criteria are ALL nit-suppressed and impact is 0. Per the calibration table `documentation` is the\nHIGHEST-QUALITY small set in the corpus (mval 0.95, drop 0.0, rev_rr 1.0). So this normalization\nwould route the best-performing small label into the nit-suppression path and start dropping those\nfindings. `sec` -> `security` carries no equivalent hazard.\nDECISION NEEDED: normalize `documentation` anyway (accepting the demotion), normalize only `sec`, or\nnormalize `documentation` while exempting it from nit-suppression. Do not implement Item 1's\n`documentation` half until this is answered.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `sec` normalizes to `security` and `documentation` to `docs` before routing/threshold lookup.\n- [ ] A label matching no known id passes through UNCHANGED — proven by a test using a project\n      criterion and an arbitrary open dimension, neither of which may be dropped or rewritten.\n- [ ] Known-id membership is resolved through the EFFECTIVE vocabulary (project overlay included),\n      not the packaged index; a test with an overlay-activated project criterion proves it.\n- [ ] No fixed enum / closed schema constraint on `criteria` is introduced anywhere.\n- [ ] ALL FIVE Item 3 sites are corrected — (A) the `sec` DET/ATTEST misclassification at lines 88\n      and 96-98, (B) the understated `security` row at 57/88, (C) the redundant\n      `project.review-phase-boundaries` threshold proposal at 78, (D) the mis-characterized and\n      under-inclusive label list at 135-136, and (E) a recorded note that\n      `calibrate_code_review_thresholds.py` reads no routing index and will reproduce (A) and (C).\n- [ ] The correction states explicitly that the four approved blocking flips and the block-impact\n      table are UNAFFECTED, so the calibration's headline result is not discarded.\n- [ ] Item 4's `documentation`/nit-suppression decision is answered on this ticket BEFORE the\n      `documentation` -> `docs` half of Item 1 is implemented.\n- [ ] `make lint` and `make typecheck` pass; existing code-review tests pass unmodified.\n"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787178621965790001,"uuid":"2f734447-7438-42a3-af8c-53ca96563aa8"}